    "tranche": "T6",
    "tranche_reason": "The paper studies multidimensional SAE features and SAE feature splitting, not neuron-pole job counts or the explanation tests named in the claim versions. Its SAE results are related context, not direct tests of those versions.",
    "gate_note": "The reported absorption and AutoInterp results concern SAE features and SASA groups, not model neurons or neuron poles, so they are recorded under versions [\"none\"]. Table 1's KL, CE, explained-variance, and sparsity metrics do not measure the claim's named outcomes. The AutoInterp scores are not tests of whether explanations predict activations on new text.",
    "test_reason": "The study proposes subspace-aware SAEs and measures SAE-latent absorption, AutoInterp and reconstruction on GPT-2 and Mistral-7B. It never audits model neurons or neuron poles, never tests InceptionV1 neurons or attribution graphs over neurons, and its AutoInterp scores concern SAE latents, not neuron explanations. Its only mention of neurons is a citation that superposition makes neuron-level interpretations fail. No version is tested.",
    "authors_conclusion": "Broadly correct. The authors argue that vector SAEs provably split multi-dimensional features, that the l1 objective prefers this splitting, and that SASA consolidates such features into single subspace groups. They report less absorption, comparable reconstruction at half the tokens, and better sample complexity. They draw no conclusions about individual model neurons.",
    "recipe": "different",
    "recipe_differences": "The paper studies vector-based SAEs and proposes subspace-aware sparse autoencoders; it does not replicate the neuron-pole audit or explanation-testing recipes in the claim versions.",
    "method_sensitivity": "The paper reports different absorption values across models and SAE architectures; Table 2 and Table 3 use different comparison setups, and Table 3 fixes GPT-2 layer 7 and matching data and token budget.",
    "fits_no_version": [
      {
        "finding": "Against standard SAEs, SASA lowers first-letter absorption on GPT-2 (6.6% vs 37.2% mean) and Mistral-7B (18.3% vs 24.0%), trained on half the token budget.",
        "proposed_claim": "Subspace (group) SAE decoders reduce feature absorption relative to vector SAEs.",
        "row_ids": [
          "r1",
          "r2",
          "r3",
